00:34:49.921 --> 00:34:52.333
but like what she wanted things to be like


00:34:52.340 --> 00:34:54.376
and then what the reality was.


00:34:55.218 --> 00:34:56.583
Not yet.


00:34:56.595 --> 00:34:58.085
We were just rehearsing.


00:34:58.096 --> 00:34:59.006
Sit down.


00:34:59.014 --> 00:34:59.844
- What are you doing--


00:34:59.848 --> 00:35:01.463
- Sit down, you little.


00:35:01.474 --> 00:35:04.011
And obviously with what
happened in their lives


00:35:04.019 --> 00:35:06.385
with John being arrested,


00:35:06.396 --> 00:35:08.933
I think that's sort of like
the culmination of it all,


00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:10.805
and along the way I'm sure


00:35:10.817 --> 00:35:11.977
that in their relationship,


00:35:11.985 --> 00:35:15.569
there were moments where
she was very much in love,


00:35:15.572 --> 00:35:20.032
but wondering, he's incredibly
driven and ambitious,


00:35:20.035 --> 00:35:22.447
and how does she fit into that picture?


00:35:22.454 --> 00:35:23.990
- Eventually the car will get built,


00:35:23.997 --> 00:35:27.285
but right now I'm trying to build a brand.


00:35:27.292 --> 00:35:29.749
- Well, it concerns me


00:35:29.753 --> 00:35:32.210
where we're gonna raise the children.


00:35:32.213 --> 00:35:35.000
- Well, wherever we set up shop,


00:35:35.008 --> 00:35:37.420
we won't have to spend much time there.


00:35:37.427 --> 00:35:38.837
All that matters is which government


00:35:38.845 --> 00:35:39.960
gives us the most money.


00:35:41.514 --> 00:35:42.720
- Well, it matters to me,


00:35:44.434 --> 00:35:45.765
and it matters to the kids.


00:35:49.606 --> 00:35:50.391
- Don't worry.


00:35:51.441 --> 00:35:53.102
It's gonna be great for all of us.


00:36:02.661 --> 00:36:05.073
-  Northern Ireland's
now experiencing rioting


00:36:05.080 --> 00:36:08.447
on a scale not seen for many years.


00:36:08.458 --> 00:36:10.574
- Why pick Belfast, Ireland,


00:36:10.585 --> 00:36:12.325
to build your motor plant?


00:36:12.337 --> 00:36:13.497
- Oh, it's very simple.


00:36:13.505 --> 00:36:15.211
Nobody anywhere in the world


00:36:15.215 --> 00:36:17.752
would come up with the
financing we needed.


00:36:17.759 --> 00:36:20.000
Now, nobody else would go to Belfast


00:36:20.011 --> 00:36:21.547
because it was such a terrible,


00:36:21.554 --> 00:36:23.419
dangerous place to be.


00:36:23.431 --> 00:36:24.887
- I read in the newspapers


00:36:24.891 --> 00:36:26.597
that John DeLorean had secured


00:36:26.601 --> 00:36:30.264
the investment he needed to
establish his car project


00:36:30.271 --> 00:36:32.432
in Northern Ireland against the background


00:36:32.440 --> 00:36:35.056
of the famous troubles


00:36:35.068 --> 00:36:37.229
that was rife at that time.


00:36:38.071 --> 00:36:40.062
-  Prolonged civil
unrest between Catholics


00:36:40.073 --> 00:36:42.780
and Protestants scares off industrialists,


00:36:42.784 --> 00:36:44.991
depriving Northern Ireland of jobs,


00:36:44.994 --> 00:36:47.610
which it needs more than
any other area of Britain.


00:36:48.540 --> 00:36:49.996
- The unemployment was insane.


00:36:49.999 --> 00:36:51.580
It was 30, 40%,


00:36:51.584 --> 00:36:55.793
and John had something to offer, jobs.


00:36:55.797 --> 00:36:58.379
The Labor government
in Britain at the time


00:36:58.383 --> 00:37:01.967
was willing to put in subsidies, loans,


00:37:01.970 --> 00:37:03.881
a piece of land,


00:37:03.888 --> 00:37:05.719
but here's the catch.


00:37:05.724 --> 00:37:07.134
It's got a population


00:37:07.142 --> 00:37:09.383
that has never built a car before.


00:37:09.394 --> 00:37:11.385
There's no pre-existing structure


00:37:11.396 --> 00:37:12.932
to build a car company there,


00:37:12.939 --> 00:37:15.897
and you have to do it in two years.


00:37:15.900 --> 00:37:18.312
I mean, are you kidding me?


00:37:18.319 --> 00:37:19.434
And all they have at this point


00:37:19.446 --> 00:37:21.482
is a hand built prototype,


00:37:21.489 --> 00:37:24.981
so it was a long list
of things to accomplish


00:37:24.993 --> 00:37:27.860
to eventually be able to
productionize the car.


00:37:27.871 --> 00:37:29.907
It became clear to everyone


00:37:29.914 --> 00:37:31.575
that they needed to have help


00:37:31.583 --> 00:37:33.039
to weed out all the problems


00:37:33.042 --> 00:37:36.000
and actually engineer this
car to be mass produced.


00:37:36.004 --> 00:37:39.246
Well, Colin Chapman
comes into the picture.


00:37:42.719 --> 00:37:45.051
- Probably the finest automobile engineer


00:37:45.054 --> 00:37:47.386
I have ever met in my life, Colin Chapman.


00:37:47.390 --> 00:37:48.971
I'll introduce Colin now.


00:37:50.101 --> 00:37:53.184
-  Colin Chapman is
the founder of Lotus Cars.


00:37:53.188 --> 00:37:56.146
He was a pioneer in the racing industry.


00:37:56.149 --> 00:37:58.390
He dominated Formula One.


00:37:58.401 --> 00:38:02.770
-  A deal had been struck
with Lotus Cars as a company


00:38:02.781 --> 00:38:05.614
and Colin Chapman as a named individual


00:38:05.617 --> 00:38:08.609
to engineer and develop DeLorean cars


00:38:08.620 --> 00:38:10.201
that could enter production.


00:38:10.205 --> 00:38:12.446
- Well, the idea of working with Lotus


00:38:12.457 --> 00:38:13.993
sounded like a good idea.


00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:15.115
We would all get together,


00:38:15.126 --> 00:38:17.412
and we'd become a joint organization


00:38:17.420 --> 00:38:21.254
and go finish the production design work.


00:38:21.257 --> 00:38:24.169
- Well, when we first got to Lotus,


00:38:24.177 --> 00:38:25.713
I mean, right out of the chute,


00:38:25.720 --> 00:38:26.960
it was kind of awkward.


00:38:26.971 --> 00:38:30.930
Here Bill and I and some
others had spent years


00:38:30.934 --> 00:38:32.925
getting us to this point,


00:38:32.936 --> 00:38:36.394
and then now it was like
Bill Collins and Bob Manion


00:38:36.397 --> 00:38:38.183
and whoever else was involved,


00:38:38.191 --> 00:38:42.104
they can kind of like just watch us,


00:38:44.280 --> 00:38:46.020
and that of course isn't
what I was gonna do,


00:38:46.032 --> 00:38:48.865
and it certainly wasn't gonna
be what Bill was gonna do.


00:38:48.868 --> 00:38:49.983
- This is Bill.


00:38:49.994 --> 00:38:51.325
Johnin?


00:38:51.329 --> 00:38:52.990
-  Bill, how the hell are you?


00:38:52.997 --> 00:38:55.283
- Look, John, I got the
contract you sent over,


00:38:56.292 --> 00:38:59.409
and I have to say it
raises some questions.


00:39:00.797 --> 00:39:03.163
You've got a lot in
here about Lotus, John,


00:39:03.174 --> 00:39:04.835
but nothing about me.


00:39:04.843 --> 00:39:05.673
- Oh, you don't have to
worry about that, Bill.


00:39:05.677 --> 00:39:07.963
It's just a lot of paperwork.


00:39:07.971 --> 00:39:09.381
You know what you're doing.


00:39:09.389 --> 00:39:10.219
- I understand that, John,


00:39:10.223 --> 00:39:11.554
but I thought this was supposed


00:39:11.558 --> 00:39:13.799
to be a collaboration on the redesign,


00:39:13.810 --> 00:39:15.516
but Chapman's nowhere to be found.


00:39:16.938 --> 00:39:19.099
- Well, they're doing a lot
of R and D, aren't they?


00:39:19.107 --> 00:39:20.768
- That's the other thing.


00:39:20.775 --> 00:39:25.109
This company in here, this GPD,


00:39:25.113 --> 00:39:26.478
I've never even heard of it.


00:39:27.532 --> 00:39:28.863
- You don't need to
worry about that, Bill.


00:39:28.867 --> 00:39:32.280
It's not going to affect you at all, okay?


00:39:32.287 --> 00:39:34.403
- Well, I don't see how that's possible.


00:39:34.414 --> 00:39:35.995
They got a hefty line item


00:39:35.999 --> 00:39:37.114
in here on engineering,


00:39:37.125 --> 00:39:39.161
but that's what we're
supposed to be doing.


00:39:39.168 --> 00:39:40.658
It just seems like you're paying twice


00:39:40.670 --> 00:39:42.661
for the same thing, John.


00:39:43.756 --> 00:39:46.293
- Well, it must be some
kind of a tax shelter, Bill.


00:39:46.301 --> 00:39:47.131
I don't know.


00:39:47.135 --> 00:39:47.965
I'm not a CPA.


00:39:47.969 --> 00:39:48.754
I'm sorry.


00:39:51.097 --> 00:39:52.462
- John, what am I doing here?


00:39:54.350 --> 00:39:55.214
Why am I even here?


00:40:01.024 --> 00:40:02.013
- I liked Bill.


00:40:02.025 --> 00:40:03.856
I think he'd done a tremendous job,


00:40:03.860 --> 00:40:07.819
but the moment I found
him reading the contract,


00:40:07.822 --> 00:40:11.440
it was quite clear that
what he'd already decided


00:40:11.451 --> 00:40:13.817
in his own mind was true,


00:40:13.828 --> 00:40:15.318
that he was out of a job.


00:40:15.330 --> 00:40:18.618
Colin Chapman was not gonna have anybody


00:40:18.625 --> 00:40:21.287
looking over Colin Chapman's shoulder.


00:40:21.294 --> 00:40:22.659
-  To do business like this,


00:40:22.670 --> 00:40:24.376
don't you have to be pretty ruthless?


00:40:26.925 --> 00:40:28.335
- I don't think you have to be ruthless.


00:40:28.343 --> 00:40:29.924
I think you have to be prepared to make


00:40:29.928 --> 00:40:32.544
some unpalatable decisions at times


00:40:32.555 --> 00:40:35.092
because frequently you're
faced with making a decision


00:40:35.099 --> 00:40:36.430
between two evils,


00:40:36.434 --> 00:40:38.345
and you're gonna hurt somebody.


00:40:38.353 --> 00:40:41.265
- John DeLorean himself, he knew.


00:40:41.272 --> 00:40:42.387
He'd arranged with Chapman


00:40:42.398 --> 00:40:45.105
that this is the way it's gonna be.


00:40:45.109 --> 00:40:49.022
Nobody had told the
guys from the U.S. team.


00:40:49.030 --> 00:40:51.692
-  It becomes
painfully clear that Bill


00:40:51.699 --> 00:40:54.281
and his engineering team are out of a job,


00:40:54.285 --> 00:40:55.650
and not only that,


00:40:55.662 --> 00:40:57.698
there's something very fishy going on


00:40:57.705 --> 00:40:59.696
with the finances of this deal.


00:40:59.707 --> 00:41:02.824
Colin Chapman was gonna
be willing to do things


00:41:02.835 --> 00:41:05.622
that Bill would never have
been comfortable with,


00:41:05.630 --> 00:41:08.747
and for John, I think that
was an important aspect


00:41:08.758 --> 00:41:10.089
to this partnership,


00:41:10.093 --> 00:41:11.128
and it was the type of thing


00:41:11.135 --> 00:41:12.796
that was so important to him at the time


00:41:12.804 --> 00:41:15.466
that he was willing to sacrifice Bill.


00:41:23.481 --> 00:41:26.939
- Dear, John, after much
deliberation on my part,


00:41:26.943 --> 00:41:28.558
I feel it is obvious to both of us


00:41:28.569 --> 00:41:30.525
that my participation in your venture


00:41:30.530 --> 00:41:32.361
is no longer the same as intended.


00:41:34.659 --> 00:41:36.115
Under the current circumstances,


00:41:36.119 --> 00:41:38.235
I feel the only alternative for me


00:41:38.246 --> 00:41:40.077
is to tender my resignation


00:41:40.081 --> 00:41:43.073
in all my present positions at this time.


00:41:43.084 --> 00:41:45.666
I wish you and the rest of
the team the best of luck.


00:41:48.047 --> 00:41:49.253
All these years later,


00:41:50.591 --> 00:41:51.797
it still looks great,


00:41:53.177 --> 00:41:55.418
and it would be nice to
have a brown interior.


00:41:58.141 --> 00:42:00.553
Well, it'd been a long, long time


00:42:00.560 --> 00:42:02.642
that we'd been working on the project,


00:42:02.645 --> 00:42:07.605
and I felt that I was being
totally undercut by John,


00:42:07.984 --> 00:42:09.815
and he really owed me a hell of lot more


00:42:09.819 --> 00:42:11.355
than to treat me the way he did,


00:42:12.530 --> 00:42:14.646
and itjust seemed like
the time to move on.


00:42:16.492 --> 00:42:18.733
-  Do you remember
having the conversation


00:42:18.745 --> 00:42:19.985
with John where you said,


00:42:19.996 --> 00:42:20.826
"I'm resigning?"


00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:22.695
- No, I don't remember.


00:42:23.833 --> 00:42:26.700
I think he was happy to see me go.


00:42:26.711 --> 00:42:27.666
-  Do you really?


00:42:27.670 --> 00:42:28.500
- Yeah.


00:42:28.504 --> 00:42:29.584
-  He didn't
try to stop you at all?


00:42:29.589 --> 00:42:30.374
- No.


00:42:36.095 --> 00:42:37.255
We're all males,


00:42:37.263 --> 00:42:38.343
but there's something to be said


00:42:38.347 --> 00:42:39.803
about being the guy that's sort of the guy


00:42:40.933 --> 00:42:43.595
that bore the baby, as it were,


00:42:43.603 --> 00:42:44.888
with the DMC-12.


00:43:04.040 --> 00:43:05.496
- Before we started filming,


00:43:05.500 --> 00:43:09.960
we knew nothing about
John DeLorean really.


00:43:14.342 --> 00:43:16.003
We basically came to the project


00:43:16.010 --> 00:43:18.467
because a British television station


00:43:18.471 --> 00:43:22.009
wanted to do something on Ireland.


00:43:22.016 --> 00:43:23.552
- And I was thinking, oh, boy,


00:43:27.105 --> 00:43:29.517
They said, "No, no, we want
it to be about automobiles


00:43:29.524 --> 00:43:30.730
"because everybody in England


00:43:30.733 --> 00:43:32.598
"just loves automobiles."


00:43:32.610 --> 00:43:34.475
-  Like most of our films,


00:43:34.487 --> 00:43:36.773
the idea was to kind of watch somebody


00:43:36.781 --> 00:43:39.363
who wants to fulfill a life dream,


00:43:39.367 --> 00:43:41.858
and that's who John was.


00:43:41.869 --> 00:43:45.236
-  John Zachary DeLorean
certainly doesn't smile much.


00:43:45.248 --> 00:43:46.454
The reason is simple.


00:43:46.457 --> 00:43:48.448
The most important project in his life


00:43:48.459 --> 00:43:50.040
is yet to be accomplished.


00:43:50.044 --> 00:43:51.580
- He was very guarded


00:43:51.587 --> 00:43:56.206
because he had so many
things, I guess, on his mind


00:43:56.217 --> 00:43:58.799
to get figured out all the time.


00:43:58.803 --> 00:44:02.045
- We've had some kind of
criticism right from day one.


00:44:02.056 --> 00:44:05.298
First, we were never gonna
get the program together.


00:44:05.309 --> 00:44:06.970
Then we'd never get the financing.


00:44:06.978 --> 00:44:08.434
Then we'd never build the plant.


00:44:08.437 --> 00:44:12.521
- I don't think the motor
car manufacturing industries,


00:44:12.525 --> 00:44:15.688
whether it be America, UK, mainland,


00:44:15.695 --> 00:44:18.903
actually ever thought
that it could be done.


00:44:18.906 --> 00:44:21.488
- Now the critics are
forecasting our failure


00:44:21.492 --> 00:44:22.902
two or three years from now,


00:44:22.910 --> 00:44:24.525
so I think as long as you can


00:44:24.537 --> 00:44:25.902
always keep your failure two


00:44:25.913 --> 00:44:27.198
or three years in the future,


00:44:27.206 --> 00:44:28.787
you're probably gonna do okay.


00:44:28.791 --> 00:44:30.452
- I think DeLorean's problem


00:44:30.459 --> 00:44:32.575
was at that time he needed cars,


00:44:32.587 --> 00:44:33.952
and he needed cars in a hurry.


00:44:33.963 --> 00:44:36.579
- You got to pick the
three or four big markets


00:44:37.425 --> 00:44:39.916
We can't wait 14 months to start selling.


00:44:39.927 --> 00:44:44.546
- It was a constant panic
of racing the clock,


00:44:44.557 --> 00:44:47.014
and it was damn the torpedoes


00:44:47.018 --> 00:44:49.225
and full speed ahead.


00:44:49.228 --> 00:44:51.685
-  It was such a
process of building the car


00:44:51.689 --> 00:44:53.225
and seeing what was working


00:44:53.232 --> 00:44:54.938
and what wasn't working.


00:44:54.942 --> 00:44:56.853
- We've had problems on
the upper control arm


00:44:56.861 --> 00:44:58.192
around the ball joint,


00:44:58.196 --> 00:45:01.313
but we also had a torsion
rod failure at 41,000 cycles.


00:45:01.324 --> 00:45:04.111
- Everybody knew that there
were gonna be problems.


00:45:04.118 --> 00:45:05.733
The view you took was, well,


00:45:05.745 --> 00:45:06.951
yeah, we expected this.


00:45:06.954 --> 00:45:08.444
Fine, let's get on and fix it.


00:45:09.498 --> 00:45:11.910
- It was crazy, absolutely crazy,


00:45:11.918 --> 00:45:13.374
but it was exciting.


00:45:13.377 --> 00:45:14.662
You were an operator on the floor


00:45:14.670 --> 00:45:16.786
seeing that lovely stainless steel car


00:45:16.797 --> 00:45:18.412
going together the way it should go.


00:45:18.424 --> 00:45:19.880
That was very rewarding.


00:45:20.801 --> 00:45:24.419
- You felt part of a family
when you went in there.


00:45:24.430 --> 00:45:26.466
There was absolutely no trouble,


00:45:26.474 --> 00:45:28.681
no Protestant, Catholic divide.


00:45:28.684 --> 00:45:31.050
- One of the proudest
achievements, I think,


00:45:31.062 --> 00:45:35.180
of DeLorean is that it was
a form of social experiment.


00:45:35.191 --> 00:45:37.807
You had for the first
time in Northern Ireland


00:45:37.818 --> 00:45:39.149
Catholics and Protestants


00:45:39.153 --> 00:45:41.064
working alongside each other.


00:45:41.072 --> 00:45:43.609
The social experiment
was beginning to work.


00:45:43.616 --> 00:45:46.904
You can have a mixed work
force and be successful.


00:45:46.911 --> 00:45:50.620
- In two years and 11 months,


00:45:50.623 --> 00:45:51.988
we had built a factory.


00:45:53.125 --> 00:45:55.411
We had designed a car.


00:45:55.419 --> 00:45:56.750
The cars were built,


00:45:56.754 --> 00:45:59.871
and that was the best time


00:45:59.882 --> 00:46:02.373
I've ever had working in any company.


00:46:04.011 --> 00:46:08.129
-  When the first
six cars came rolling out,


00:46:08.140 --> 00:46:09.676
the news crews were all there.


00:46:10.643 --> 00:46:11.974
John was the biggest hero


00:46:11.978 --> 00:46:13.343
in Northern Ireland that day.


00:46:14.355 --> 00:46:16.971
- It's incredible that he got 3,500 cars


00:46:16.983 --> 00:46:19.065
into the United States on a boat,


00:46:19.068 --> 00:46:21.184
and we actually sailed
on the damn boat too


00:46:22.113 --> 00:46:26.152
because I just was so
excited to see that happen


00:46:26.158 --> 00:46:30.401
'cause I knew he showed
them that he could do it.


00:46:30.413 --> 00:46:31.243
-  Buyers are reported to be


00:46:31.247 --> 00:46:32.737
queuing up in the United States


00:46:32.748 --> 00:46:35.615
for the company's
futuristic Goldwing DMC-12.


00:46:35.626 --> 00:46:38.333
-  And D.A. and Chris,


00:46:38.337 --> 00:46:39.417
you watch their movie,


00:46:39.422 --> 00:46:41.253
and the perception you get at the end


00:46:41.257 --> 00:46:43.168
is this is a success.


00:46:43.175 --> 00:46:45.086
- Everything was hunky dOI‘Y:


00:46:45.094 --> 00:46:46.925
and it was great, you know?


00:46:46.929 --> 00:46:49.090
We'd succeeded in October '81.


00:46:56.314 --> 00:46:58.054
-  How is it?


00:46:58.065 --> 00:47:01.228
- DeLorean is four or five little moves


00:47:01.235 --> 00:47:03.021
away from victory.


00:47:04.405 --> 00:47:06.316
He's on the doorstep of that victory.


00:47:08.284 --> 00:47:11.026
In the music of it, it's like Bolero.


00:47:12.413 --> 00:47:13.619
John just says .


00:47:18.461 --> 00:47:20.042
We don't migrate from that.


00:47:20.963 --> 00:47:23.204
He's played a pretty high stakes game,


00:47:23.215 --> 00:47:25.877
and he really, really stays calm


00:47:25.885 --> 00:47:27.876
and very contained,


00:47:27.887 --> 00:47:30.469
and the drama is in him being contained


00:47:30.473 --> 00:47:33.010
while everything collapsed around him.


00:47:34.185 --> 00:47:36.392
- Things started to go south very quickly.


00:47:37.355 --> 00:47:42.224
Around the fall of 1981,


00:47:43.486 --> 00:47:45.898
there was a series of monumental cockups.


00:47:45.905 --> 00:47:48.112
-  So everything's going
really smoothly, but it's--


00:47:48.115 --> 00:47:50.151
- Well, I wouldn't say smoothly.


00:47:50.159 --> 00:47:52.491
I don't want to give the appearance


00:47:52.495 --> 00:47:56.079
that everything's just absolutely perfect.


00:47:57.041 --> 00:47:59.703
- When those cars first came off the boat,


00:47:59.710 --> 00:48:02.577
they were just a product
warranty nightmare.


00:48:02.588 --> 00:48:03.543
- The door won't close.


00:48:03.547 --> 00:48:04.753
The gaps are all wrong.


00:48:04.757 --> 00:48:06.668
The window wiper won't work.


00:48:06.675 --> 00:48:09.041
The door mechanism won't work.


00:48:09.053 --> 00:48:09.883
That happened.


00:48:09.887 --> 00:48:11.548
It happened to me once,
getting stuck in the car.


00:48:11.555 --> 00:48:13.637
Couldn't open the door, you know?


00:48:15.017 --> 00:48:16.302
- We reviewed the car.


00:48:17.311 --> 00:48:19.393
It COStS more than a corvette.


00:48:19.397 --> 00:48:20.637
It didn't handle that great.


00:48:20.648 --> 00:48:22.058
It was kind of slow.


00:48:22.066 --> 00:48:25.854
-  It was not the car he promised,


00:48:25.861 --> 00:48:30.195
this fuel efficient, high performance car.


00:48:30.199 --> 00:48:32.064
- It started to go south


00:48:32.076 --> 00:48:35.113
when this happened in the teeth


00:48:35.121 --> 00:48:38.784
of a diminishing economy.


00:48:38.791 --> 00:48:40.452
-  Now the dream seems to be fading.


00:48:40.459 --> 00:48:42.916
Sales are way down in the United States


00:48:42.920 --> 00:48:44.581
perhaps for economic reasons,


00:48:44.588 --> 00:48:47.546
perhaps because of some
dissatisfaction with the car.


00:48:47.550 --> 00:48:50.758
- In a scenario where
there's a few thousand cars


00:48:50.761 --> 00:48:52.251
coming to the U.S.,


00:48:52.263 --> 00:48:54.345
there's these quality control issues.


00:48:54.348 --> 00:48:57.306
The lesson that should
have been learned from this


00:48:57.309 --> 00:49:00.176
is let's slow production
down a little bit more.


00:49:00.187 --> 00:49:01.267
Let's figure out a way


00:49:01.272 --> 00:49:03.103
to work out all these issues.


00:49:03.107 --> 00:49:05.894
Unfortunately, that's the opposite


00:49:05.901 --> 00:49:07.562
of what John decided to do.


00:49:07.570 --> 00:49:10.983
He pulled the trigger on
basically doubling production.


00:49:10.990 --> 00:49:12.480
-  And what happened?


00:49:12.491 --> 00:49:17.451
These cars were backing up
like plague rats on the docks.


00:49:17.997 --> 00:49:20.739
- And it was just the wrong, wrong move,


00:49:20.749 --> 00:49:21.864
so the question has to be,


00:49:21.876 --> 00:49:23.741
well, why did he do this?


00:49:23.752 --> 00:49:25.117
It was a typical John move


00:49:25.129 --> 00:49:28.041
because he saw a little angle


00:49:28.048 --> 00:49:31.006
in the contract where
if you upped the amount


00:49:31.010 --> 00:49:34.002
of people that were working
there and increased jobs,


00:49:34.013 --> 00:49:34.843
then the government was


00:49:34.847 --> 00:49:37.304
supposed to be giving
him an infusion of cash,


00:49:37.308 --> 00:49:38.969
and so he thought he
was gonna take advantage


00:49:38.976 --> 00:49:40.557
of this little clause.


00:49:40.561 --> 00:49:42.847
- Anyone who deals with
the government knows


00:49:42.855 --> 00:49:45.562
that that's a risk in itself.


00:49:45.566 --> 00:49:46.931
What if the politics change?


00:49:46.942 --> 00:49:48.898
What if the administration changes?


00:49:49.820 --> 00:49:52.687
Well, Margaret Thatcher happened.


00:49:54.492 --> 00:49:56.483
- We'd had a change of government by now.


00:49:56.494 --> 00:49:58.405
The Conservatives were in power.


00:49:58.412 --> 00:50:02.997
Margaret Thatcher, the arch
privateer, was prime minister.


00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:05.616
Between Margaret Thatcher
as prime minister and us,


00:50:05.628 --> 00:50:06.959
there was a secretary of state


00:50:06.962 --> 00:50:09.624
for Northern Ireland, James Prior.


00:50:09.632 --> 00:50:12.214
- Well, I suppose my role with DeLorean


00:50:12.218 --> 00:50:15.176
was to keep him under control.


00:50:15.179 --> 00:50:18.637
The Labor government had started
off the DeLorean business.


00:50:18.641 --> 00:50:21.929
The Conservative government
took a tougher line


00:50:21.936 --> 00:50:26.521
towards subsidies for Northern
Ireland, for DeLorean.


00:50:26.524 --> 00:50:28.685
-  DeLorean wanted
another $76 million


00:50:28.692 --> 00:50:30.102
from the British.


00:50:30.110 --> 00:50:32.442
If not, he said, "There would be layoffs."


00:50:32.446 --> 00:50:35.028
- I don't care about the
British taxpayers' money


00:50:35.032 --> 00:50:35.862
or my own money.


00:50:35.866 --> 00:50:37.072
I think the most important thing


00:50:37.076 --> 00:50:38.987
is we have some people there.


00:50:38.994 --> 00:50:41.610
This is a very, very
important part of their life,


00:50:41.622 --> 00:50:43.328
and nothing in the world


00:50:43.332 --> 00:50:45.243
should be permitted to interfere with it.


00:50:45.251 --> 00:50:48.118
- John overestimated the fact


00:50:48.128 --> 00:50:51.211
that we had created jobs


00:50:51.215 --> 00:50:54.207
and that it was the jobs that would secure


00:50:55.427 --> 00:50:58.339
even more money to keep us alive.


00:50:58.347 --> 00:51:01.089
That was not the way
Margaret Thatcher saw it.


00:51:01.100 --> 00:51:03.341
- She genuinely took the view


00:51:03.352 --> 00:51:04.592
that we couldn't go on


00:51:04.603 --> 00:51:06.889
pouring a lot of money after bad,


00:51:07.856 --> 00:51:09.471
and in the end, we had to say no.


00:51:10.943 --> 00:51:12.149
- The result?


00:51:12.152 --> 00:51:13.437
We ran out of cash.


00:51:14.446 --> 00:51:17.529
Therefore, we were heading for insolvency,


00:51:18.492 --> 00:51:19.902
and that's at the point


00:51:19.910 --> 00:51:22.367
when things got very serious indeed.


00:51:26.041 --> 00:51:27.747
-  We have a group right now


00:51:27.751 --> 00:51:28.911
that has got the ability


00:51:28.919 --> 00:51:32.252
to come up with for sure 30 million


00:51:32.256 --> 00:51:33.962
and upwards of there.


00:51:35.718 --> 00:51:37.879
- And during this whole process,


00:51:37.886 --> 00:51:40.252
John's back was really
up against the wall.


00:51:40.264 --> 00:51:41.424
He had to figure out a way


00:51:41.432 --> 00:51:42.888
to pay some of the money back


00:51:42.891 --> 00:51:43.721
to the British government


00:51:43.726 --> 00:51:45.387
as well as present to them


00:51:45.394 --> 00:51:47.885
a feasible way to keep the company open


00:51:47.896 --> 00:51:49.386
and make it profitable,


00:51:49.398 --> 00:51:50.888
or else they were threatening


00:51:53.402 --> 00:51:54.812
liquidate the assets,


00:51:54.820 --> 00:51:56.606
and everyone was gonna be out of a job.


00:51:56.614 --> 00:51:58.195
-  Today DeLorean said he is looking


00:51:58.198 --> 00:51:59.608
for outside financing.


00:51:59.617 --> 00:52:01.448
-  All I want to do
is keep the factory open


00:52:01.452 --> 00:52:03.363
so that people can work, nothing else.


00:52:03.370 --> 00:52:05.110
-  You last said that
you'd keep the factory open


00:52:05.122 --> 00:52:06.487
if it was your last breath.


00:52:06.498 --> 00:52:07.704
Do you still stand by that?


00:52:07.708 --> 00:52:08.868
-  I'm still trying.


00:52:08.876 --> 00:52:10.958
- During the months of
sort of August, September,


00:52:10.961 --> 00:52:15.204
we'd had periodic contact
with John DeLorean.


00:52:15.215 --> 00:52:19.959
He on a regular basis was coming
up with potential funders.


00:52:19.970 --> 00:52:21.255
-  It's been a
lot of time and effort,


00:52:21.263 --> 00:52:22.378
but I think we're now just


00:52:22.389 --> 00:52:27.099
starting out on a very
successful relationship.


00:52:27.102 --> 00:52:27.932
-  Oh, I think so, too.


00:52:27.936 --> 00:52:29.426
-  It just took a
while to put it together,


00:52:29.438 --> 00:52:30.268
and that's all.


00:52:30.272 --> 00:52:34.015
- The workers and the
people closest to him at DMC


00:52:34.026 --> 00:52:34.856
were being told,


00:52:34.860 --> 00:52:36.725
"Hey, listen, everything's gonna be fine.


00:52:36.737 --> 00:52:39.649
"I definitely have the
sources coming through."


00:52:39.657 --> 00:52:42.899
- And we were still at the stage


00:52:42.910 --> 00:52:45.151
where we were hoping and hoping


00:52:45.162 --> 00:52:46.993
that it would go all right.


00:52:46.997 --> 00:52:48.737
-  In terms of having that,


00:52:48.749 --> 00:52:50.364
the first 10 million,


00:52:50.376 --> 00:52:53.493
it sounds like that's
gonna be Tuesday, right?


00:52:53.504 --> 00:52:54.584
-  I would say I should be able


00:52:54.588 --> 00:52:56.374
to get that going very quick.


00:52:56.382 --> 00:52:58.168
-  The impression
we got at that time


00:52:58.175 --> 00:52:59.164
was that John had pretty well


00:52:59.176 --> 00:53:04.091
gotten the money by the
17th of October, 1982.


00:53:26.704 --> 00:53:27.489
- Hello?


00:53:31.542 --> 00:53:32.327
What?


00:53:41.552 --> 00:53:44.259
What are you talking about?


00:53:44.263 --> 00:53:48.256
- We were in the city in
the apartment at 8:34.


00:53:49.727 --> 00:53:50.682
The phone rings.


00:53:52.187 --> 00:53:53.302
Mom, who was that?


00:53:54.606 --> 00:53:58.144
And one phone call,


00:53:59.611 --> 00:54:01.397
my life changed forever.


00:54:02.489 --> 00:54:04.354
-  John DeLorean
was arrested yesterday


00:54:04.366 --> 00:54:06.527
after he met with undercover drug agents


00:54:06.535 --> 00:54:07.866
who DeLorean apparently thought


00:54:07.870 --> 00:54:08.825
were going to help him make


00:54:08.829 --> 00:54:11.195
a huge profit in a cocaine deal


00:54:11.206 --> 00:54:13.868
involving more than 100 kilos of cocaine.


00:54:13.876 --> 00:54:16.037
DeLorean's wife, Cristina Ferrare,


00:54:16.044 --> 00:54:17.079
an actress and model,


00:54:17.087 --> 00:54:19.294
arrived in Los Angeles early this morning.


00:54:19.298 --> 00:54:20.128
- Wait a minute.


00:54:20.132 --> 00:54:21.167
Wait a minute.


00:54:21.175 --> 00:54:23.211
I just found out a few hours ago.


00:54:23.218 --> 00:54:24.333
I know nothing.


00:54:24.344 --> 00:54:25.504
I caught a plane.


00:54:25.512 --> 00:54:26.342
I'm here.


00:54:26.346 --> 00:54:27.176
I'm--


00:54:27.181 --> 00:54:28.671
- Everybody was in total shock.


00:54:28.682 --> 00:54:31.549
Nowhere in our wildest dreams did we think


00:54:31.560 --> 00:54:33.346
anything like this would occur.


00:54:33.353 --> 00:54:35.218
-  John DeLorean
may be the perfect proof


00:54:35.230 --> 00:54:36.185
of the old saying,


00:54:36.190 --> 00:54:37.270
"The bigger they come,


00:54:37.274 --> 00:54:38.764
"the harder they fall,"


00:54:38.776 --> 00:54:39.606
and we might add,


00:54:39.610 --> 00:54:41.692
"the more people want to hear about it."


00:54:41.695 --> 00:54:42.901
- It was just disbelief,


00:54:44.448 --> 00:54:47.440
and then the news just progressively


00:54:47.451 --> 00:54:48.816
got worse and worse and worse.


00:54:48.827 --> 00:54:52.615
- He was expressing an
interest in financing


00:54:52.623 --> 00:54:54.284
some type of operation


00:54:54.291 --> 00:54:58.125
that would produce quickly
large sums of money


00:54:58.128 --> 00:55:00.369
on return from the investment.


00:55:00.380 --> 00:55:03.713
- I was shocked out of my socks


00:55:04.676 --> 00:55:07.884
to see him there in this position that,


00:55:07.888 --> 00:55:09.549
I'd never seen him in anything


00:55:09.556 --> 00:55:12.298
except being pretty much
on top of the world.


00:55:12.309 --> 00:55:14.015
- The sensational case involves


00:55:14.019 --> 00:55:16.351
more than 200 pounds of cocaine,


00:55:16.355 --> 00:55:18.346
an elaborate undercover investigation,


00:55:18.357 --> 00:55:20.348
the end of the DeLorean car,


00:55:20.359 --> 00:55:21.895
and much, much more.


00:55:28.116 --> 00:55:30.858
- It's been said, it's a cliche now,


00:55:30.869 --> 00:55:33.736
that the dream had
turned into a nightmare.


00:55:34.998 --> 00:55:36.954
Could you imagine how we felt


00:55:36.959 --> 00:55:38.824
seeing John DeLorean,


00:55:38.836 --> 00:55:42.124
our angel, I feel like?


00:55:42.130 --> 00:55:43.870
There he was going away,


00:55:43.882 --> 00:55:46.498
and everybody was downtrodden


00:55:46.510 --> 00:55:50.799
because we felt sure
that he had done so much.


00:55:50.806 --> 00:55:52.671
He'd come through again,


00:55:52.683 --> 00:55:55.516
but this time, unfortunately, he didn't.


00:55:57.020 --> 00:55:58.055
-  Well, you're
walking out of the plant


00:55:58.063 --> 00:55:58.893
for the last time today.


00:55:58.897 --> 00:55:59.727
How do you feel about it?


00:55:59.731 --> 00:56:00.561
- We got nothing.


00:56:00.566 --> 00:56:01.976
- Pretty upset.


00:56:01.984 --> 00:56:03.474
-  What're your chances
of getting another job now?


00:56:03.485 --> 00:56:04.395
- None at all.


00:56:04.403 --> 00:56:05.643
-  Not very good at all.


00:56:05.654 --> 00:56:07.986
- I had to go into the plant that morning,


00:56:07.990 --> 00:56:09.571
call the people together.


00:56:09.575 --> 00:56:11.782
They'd all heard the
same thing, of course,


00:56:12.828 --> 00:56:14.693
and it really was the end.


00:56:15.998 --> 00:56:18.535
- I was going around with a auctioneer,


00:56:18.542 --> 00:56:21.158
and he was putting tags


00:56:21.169 --> 00:56:22.534
on every piece of equipment,


00:56:24.047 --> 00:56:25.332
and that was my last day.


00:56:27.759 --> 00:56:29.795
- The expectation of everybody


00:56:29.803 --> 00:56:31.339
working their hearts out over there


00:56:31.346 --> 00:56:33.678
to get these cars built
to a good standard,


00:56:33.682 --> 00:56:37.095
and they felt it was gonna be their life,


00:56:39.187 --> 00:56:40.017
and it all fell apart,


00:56:40.022 --> 00:56:41.762
and it was incredibly sad.


00:56:45.277 --> 00:56:47.017
- I meet up with people now and then


00:56:47.029 --> 00:56:51.068
that either they worked there


00:56:51.074 --> 00:56:52.063
or their dad worked there


00:56:52.075 --> 00:56:54.191
or their granddad worked there,


00:56:54.202 --> 00:56:55.317
and you get the same questions.


00:56:55.329 --> 00:56:56.159
"What was it really like?"


00:56:56.163 --> 00:56:59.405
It was the best job that we ever had,


00:56:59.416 --> 00:57:01.407
and it was the worst day of their lives


00:57:01.418 --> 00:57:03.204
when it finally closed.


00:57:08.175 --> 00:57:09.915
-  After entering
a plea of not guilty


00:57:09.927 --> 00:57:12.009
to all nine drug charges against him,


00:57:12.012 --> 00:57:14.003
John DeLorean came out
of the federal courthouse


00:57:14.014 --> 00:57:15.720
in Los Angeles with his wife,


00:57:15.724 --> 00:57:17.339
model Cristina Ferrare,


00:57:17.351 --> 00:57:18.386
and the four lawyers


00:57:18.393 --> 00:57:20.258
who are now putting together his defense.


00:57:20.270 --> 00:57:22.056
DeLorean said nothing today,


00:57:22.064 --> 00:57:23.725
but he is reported to
have told his lawyers


00:57:23.732 --> 00:57:25.848
he was desperate to save his company.


00:57:25.859 --> 00:57:27.565
DeLorean's lawyers are preparing to argue


00:57:27.569 --> 00:57:30.060
the government entrapped DeLorean.


00:57:30.072 --> 00:57:30.982
-  Give him some room, guys.


00:57:30.989 --> 00:57:31.819
Give him some room.


00:57:31.823 --> 00:57:32.653
Come on.


00:57:32.658 --> 00:57:35.616
- It was the case of the
century for that year


00:57:36.536 --> 00:57:38.492
'cause every year has
a case of the century,


00:57:38.497 --> 00:57:40.283
trial of the century,


00:57:40.290 --> 00:57:43.874
so we were the trial of
the century for 1984.


00:57:43.877 --> 00:57:45.663
-  The sidewalks in
front of the courthouse


00:57:45.671 --> 00:57:47.161
will be jammed with reporters


00:57:47.172 --> 00:57:49.379
and camera crews from all over the world.


00:57:49.383 --> 00:57:50.964
- Unless you were there and you lived it,


00:57:50.968 --> 00:57:52.833
you don't know how captivating


00:57:52.844 --> 00:57:55.085
it was for the nation.


00:57:55.097 --> 00:57:56.428
-  A young entrepreneur started


00:57:56.431 --> 00:57:58.217
hanging around the courthouse steps today


00:57:58.225 --> 00:57:59.681
hawking buttons that say,


00:57:59.685 --> 00:58:02.176
"John 2. DeLorean Cocaine
Trafficking Trial,


00:58:02.187 --> 00:58:03.893
"Los Angeles, '84."


00:58:03.897 --> 00:58:08.391
- Our public views these
as if it's a bad guy,


00:58:08.402 --> 00:58:11.144
get them, and if it's not a bad guy,


00:58:11.154 --> 00:58:12.064
get the government.


00:58:12.072 --> 00:58:14.859
It's all really kind of
entertainment to the public.


00:58:14.866 --> 00:58:17.824
- John DeLorean had some bad news today.


00:58:18.954 --> 00:58:21.912
The Automobile Association
named him dealer of the year.


00:58:25.252 --> 00:58:26.742
- A businessman down on his luck


00:58:26.753 --> 00:58:28.618
tries to make money in a dope deal.


00:58:28.630 --> 00:58:32.589
I mean, that's the John
DeLorean that I knew.


00:58:32.592 --> 00:58:33.422
-  Okay, you know how--


00:58:33.427 --> 00:58:35.668
-  The case was
of overwhelming strength.


00:58:36.513 --> 00:58:38.219
Here's the cocaine,


00:58:38.223 --> 00:58:39.963
and here's your target,


00:58:39.975 --> 00:58:43.467
and he's holding up a glass
of champagne, toasting.


00:58:44.563 --> 00:58:46.303
- To a lot of success for everybody.


00:58:46.314 --> 00:58:48.805
- And says that this is better than,


00:58:48.817 --> 00:58:49.647
what was it?


00:58:49.651 --> 00:58:51.437
Better than something.


00:58:51.445 --> 00:58:52.480
- Is it better than gold?


00:58:53.321 --> 00:58:55.812
Gold weighs more than
that, for God's sakes.


00:58:55.824 --> 00:58:58.406
- Everybody believed that the events


00:58:58.410 --> 00:59:00.241
were showing John DeLorean


00:59:00.245 --> 00:59:02.406
in the middle of a drug deal.


00:59:02.414 --> 00:59:03.745
One of the things I asked


00:59:03.749 --> 00:59:05.705
the jurors very early on was,


00:59:05.709 --> 00:59:07.870
would it surprise you if I told you


00:59:07.878 --> 00:59:09.789
you weren't seeing a drug deal?


00:59:09.796 --> 00:59:12.583
And just hang on and
watch the whole movie,


00:59:12.591 --> 00:59:14.206
and at the end,


00:59:14.217 --> 00:59:17.880
you're gonna see it isn't
what it appeared to be,


00:59:17.888 --> 00:59:20.004
a real thriller, of course,


00:59:20.015 --> 00:59:21.300
which it turned out to be.


00:59:28.982 --> 00:59:33.851
- The case starts with a
guy named James Hoffman.


00:59:33.862 --> 00:59:36.399
He was the informant.


00:59:36.406 --> 00:59:40.570
He was somebody who had been
a major narcotics trafficker


00:59:40.577 --> 00:59:44.069
who had the bad luck of being caught,


00:59:44.081 --> 00:59:46.572
and he decided to cooperate.


